---
import Document from "@/layouts/Document.astro";
import { LAB, OPERATOR, REPO } from "@/lib/site";
---

<Document
  title="Privacy"
  heading="Privacy note"
  description="letsauth.eu sets no cookies and collects no personal data beyond cookieless, aggregate EU analytics. What is processed, by whom, and your rights under the GDPR."
  path="/privacy/"
  eyebrow="Legal"
  lede="letsauth.eu sets no cookies, has no accounts and does not track you across sites. It counts page views with cookieless, aggregate analytics hosted in the EU."
  updated="2026-10-22"
>
  <h2>In short</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No cookies, no fingerprinting, no advertising, no social media embeds.</li>
    <li>No personal information is collected, sold, shared with advertisers or used for profiling.</li>
    <li>Fonts, scripts and styles come from this site. Your browser contacts no third party to show a page.</li>
    <li>
      The one thing stored in your browser is your colour theme, and only if you choose one. It
      never leaves your device.
    </li>
    <li>
      Nothing is scanned on a visitor's request. A scheduled backend job reads the public
      metadata of listed servers. It involves no data about you.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>The controller under the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) is:</p>
  <address class="panel">
    <strong>{OPERATOR.name}</strong><br />
    {LAB}<br />
    {OPERATOR.street}<br />
    {`${OPERATOR.postcode} ${OPERATOR.city}, ${OPERATOR.country}`}<br />
    <a href={`mailto:${OPERATOR.email}`}>{OPERATOR.email}</a>
  </address>

  <h2>Hosting and delivery</h2>
  <p>
    Bunny.net (BunnyWay d.o.o., Ljubljana, Slovenia) serves the site as static files from a
    storage zone in the EU. To deliver a page, its servers must process your IP address, the
    requested address, the time and the browser identification your browser sends. Bunny.net processes this on our behalf under a data processing agreement and
    may keep it in short-lived technical logs for security and operation. We do not use it to
    identify or profile visitors.
  </p>
  <p>Legal basis: our legitimate interest in delivering the site securely (Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR).</p>

  <h2>Analytics</h2>
  <p>
    We count visits with <a href="https://plausible.io/data-policy" rel="noopener">Plausible
    Analytics</a> (Plausible Insights OÜ, Tartu, Estonia). It works without cookies or personal
    data and stores its data in the EU. For each page view it records:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Page address</strong>, to see which pages are read and how often.</li>
    <li><strong>Referring site</strong>, to see where readers come from.</li>
    <li><strong>Browser and operating system</strong>, by name only, read from the browser identification, which is then discarded.</li>
    <li><strong>Device type</strong>, derived from the window width, which is then discarded.</li>
    <li><strong>Country</strong>, looked up from the IP address, which is then discarded.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It also records when a link to another site or a data export is followed (the link
    address), and when a page is not found (its address). Your browser sends these counts to
    letsauth.eu, and Bunny.net forwards them to Plausible with your IP address, which Plausible
    uses as described below and does not store. A page your browser loads ahead of time, in case
    you open it next, is counted only if you do open it.
  </p>
  <p>
    To count unique visitors per day without a cookie, Plausible combines the IP address and
    browser identification with a salt that changes every 24 hours and keeps only the resulting
    hash. The salt is then deleted, so nobody can recognise a visitor the next day or across
    sites.
    IP addresses are never stored. The statistics are aggregate and nobody can trace them back
    to you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Legal basis: our legitimate interest in knowing which pages are useful (Art. 6 (1) (f)
    GDPR). Because nothing is stored on or read from your device, no consent is needed under
    § 25 of the German Telecommunications and Digital Services Data Protection Act (TDDDG).
  </p>

  <h2>What your browser stores</h2>
  <p>
    If you use the theme switch, the choice (light or dark) is saved in your browser's local
    storage under the key <code>theme</code> so the next page opens in the same theme. It is
    never sent to us or anyone else. You can delete it at any time in your browser's site data
    settings. Storing it is strictly necessary for a function you asked for
    (§ 25 (2) no. 2 TDDDG).
  </p>
  <p>Pages read fully without JavaScript. Nothing else is stored.</p>

  <h2>Scanning of MCP servers</h2>
  <p>
    letsauth scans nothing on a visitor's request, and no form can start a scan. Once a night, a
    scheduled job on the project's own backend (Scaleway, EU region) reads the public metadata of
    the MCP servers listed in the atlas. These are the documents a server publishes for any
    client to read. The job uses no credentials and starts no authorization flow. Its findings
    are published as data in the <a href={REPO} rel="noopener">public repository</a>. The job
    processes technical data about services and no personal data about visitors. Server
    maintainers can ask for their server to be withdrawn, as <a href="/contribute/">the
    contribute page</a> describes.
  </p>

  <h2>Contributions and GitHub</h2>
  <p>
    Corrections and new data arrive as issues and pull requests on GitHub (GitHub, Inc., USA).
    When you contribute there, GitHub's{" "}
    <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" rel="noopener">privacy statement</a>{" "}
    applies. The handle you give in an observation (<code>observedBy</code>) becomes part of the
    public dataset under CC BY 4.0, which requires attribution. If you want it removed, write to
    us and we will act on your request under Art. 17 GDPR. Copies in the public history of the
    repository, or copies others made, may remain.
  </p>
  <p>Links to GitHub and to specification publishers are plain links. Nothing loads from them until you follow one.</p>

  <h2>Email</h2>
  <p>
    If you write to us, we use your address and message only to answer you, and delete them
    when the conversation is over unless the law requires us to keep them. Legal basis:
    Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR, or Art. 6 (1) (b) GDPR where your message is about a contract.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>Under the GDPR you have the right to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>access the personal data we hold about you (Art. 15)</li>
    <li>have it corrected (Art. 16) or erased (Art. 17)</li>
    <li>restrict its processing (Art. 18) and receive it in a portable format (Art. 20)</li>
    <li>object to processing based on legitimate interest (Art. 21)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Write to <a href={`mailto:${OPERATOR.email}`}>{OPERATOR.email}</a>. You can also complain
    to a data protection authority. Ours is the{" "}
    <a href="https://www.datenschutz-berlin.de/" rel="noopener">Berlin Commissioner for Data
    Protection and Freedom of Information</a> (Berliner Beauftragte für Datenschutz und
    Informationsfreiheit).
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    When the site starts processing data in a new way, this note changes first. Its history is
    public in the <a href={`${REPO}/commits/main/src/pages/privacy.astro`} rel="noopener">repository</a>.
  </p>
</Document>

